Since this work has been carried out intermittently, I have not written the code in a very standardized way. Here is a simple readme.

# The environment is easy to install ,just pip install the lacking packages the ide tells you.
# each json files in ./configs is a strategy. human means that you can play a game vs other strategies with command lines.
# eg. The state is as follow:
# --------------------------------------------------
# Player1 hand cards: [[1, 1], [0, 0], [0, 1], [2, 3], [2, 6]]
# Player2 hand cards: 2
# Board: [[3, 5], [5, 5], [5, 4], [4, 0], [0, 5], [5, 2], [2, 2], [2, 4], [4, 3], [3, 3], [3, 6], [6, 5], [5, 1], [1, 4], [4, 6], [6, 6]]
#Stock: 5
# --------------------------------------------------
# Choose the card you want to play and position: left side 3, right side 4, input: card@position, e.g. [3, 3]@3
# [2, 6]@4
# [2, 6] is one of your cards. @3 means that you want to the card next to the left side of the Board. @4 means the right side.
# The state is as follows:
# --------------------------------------------------
# Player1 cards: [[1, 1], [0, 0], [0, 1], [2, 3], [2, 6]]
# Player2 cards: 2
# Board: [[3, 5], [5, 5], [5, 4], [4, 0], [0, 5], [5, 2], [2, 2], [2, 4], [4, 3], [3, 3], [3, 6], [6, 5], [5, 1], [1, 4], [4, 6], [6, 6]]
# Stock: 5
# --------------------------------------------------
# Choose the card you want to play and position: left side is 3, right side is 4, input format: card@position, e.g. [3, 3]@3

# game/game_local.py monitors a game between strategies. you can use the following command to play with a SLModel policy.
# also you can change different strategies by change the config file.
python game/game_local.py -policy1_config ./configs/human.json -policy2_config ./configs/SLModel.json -game_num 1000 -print_details

# ./dominos_ai/TrainSLModel.py is the SLModel implement.The model predicts the action scores under states. Training samples are from ramdom vs ramdom, random select a state and the final score is the regression target.

./dominos_ai/TrainActionAndStateValueModel.py is not finished, so it may not work.